### Vulnerable Code Snippet

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class CommandExecutor {
    public void executeCommand(String userInput) {
        // This method executes a system command based on user input.
        try {
            // vulnerable: Directly using user input in Runtime.exec() can lead to command injection
            Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(userInput);
            BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CommandExecutor executor = new CommandExecutor();
        // Suppose we get userInput from a web form or similar source
        String userInput = "ls; rm -rf /"; // Hypothetical malicious input
        executor.executeCommand(userInput);
    }
}
```

***

### Fixed Code Snippet

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class SafeCommandExecutor {
    public void executeSafeCommand(String... commands) {
        // This method executes a system command safely by passing individual arguments
        try {
            ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(commands);
            Process process = pb.start();
            BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SafeCommandExecutor executor = new SafeCommandExecutor();
        // Instead of passing a single string, pass each argument separately
        executor.executeSafeCommand("ls"); // Safe usage
    }
}
```

In the first code snippet, the `executeCommand` method is vulnerable because it directly uses user input to construct a command, which can be exploited if the input contains malicious commands. In the second code snippet, the `executeSafeCommand` method mitigates this risk by accepting individual command arguments, thus preventing command injection attacks.

